android: use the monotonic clock from libc instead of dlopen'ing librt

os::Linux::clock_init() resolves clock_gettime()/clock_getres() through
dlopen("librt.so.1"), falling back to dlopen("librt.so"). Neither name
resolves on bionic, where both functions live in libc, so the lookup always
failed: the VM stayed on gettimeofday() and printed

  No monotonic clock was available - timed services may be adversely
  affected if the time-of-day clock changes

on stderr for every process it started. That warning is the first line of
`java -version 2>&1`, while common/autoconf/boot-jdk.m4 takes head -n1 of
exactly that output to decide the boot JDK version, so a JDK built from this
tree could not even be used as its own boot JDK. Command-line tools that
parse `java -version` are affected the same way.

bionic exposes both through libc at the API level this port targets, so on
Android call them directly and keep the dlopen path for glibc.

diff --git a/hotspot/src/os/linux/vm/os_linux.cpp b/hotspot/src/os/linux/vm/os_linux.cpp
--- a/hotspot/src/os/linux/vm/os_linux.cpp
+++ b/hotspot/src/os/linux/vm/os_linux.cpp
@@ -1404,6 +1404,18 @@
 #endif
 
 void os::Linux::clock_init() {
+#ifdef __ANDROID__
+  // bionic resolves clock_gettime() and clock_getres() through libc and has
+  // no librt.so.1 to dlopen, so the lookup below always fails here and the VM
+  // falls back to gettimeofday(), warning about it on every single run.
+  struct timespec res;
+  struct timespec tp;
+  if (::clock_getres(CLOCK_MONOTONIC, &res) == 0 &&
+      ::clock_gettime(CLOCK_MONOTONIC, &tp)  == 0) {
+    _clock_gettime = ::clock_gettime;
+    return;
+  }
+#else
   // we do dlopen's in this particular order due to bug in linux
   // dynamical loader (see 6348968) leading to crash on exit
   void* handle = dlopen("librt.so.1", RTLD_LAZY);
@@ -1438,6 +1450,7 @@
       }
     }
   }
+#endif
   warning("No monotonic clock was available - timed services may " \
           "be adversely affected if the time-of-day clock changes");
 }
